  "response": "오늘 이 이야기를 꺼내기까지 꽤 오래 머릿속에서 돌리고 계셨을 것 같아요. \"외롭다\"는 말 자체가 어렵다고 하셨는데, 막상 이렇게 구체적으로 꺼내놓으시니까 그 안에 여러 겹이 들어 있다는 게 느껴져요. 일터가 사라지면 사람들도 함께 사라질 것 같은 두려움, 모임에 가도 어울리지 못할 것 같다는 예상, 그리고 아들의 질문이 오래 남았다는 것까지요.\n\n한 가지만 여쭤볼게요. 모임에 가려다 물러서게 되는 그 순간이 있을 텐데, 그때 몸이나 생각에서 어떤 신호가 먼저 와요?",
  "reasoning": "The user is a client who has shared several important things:\n\n1. Their museum contract might end - a practical concern about work\n2. Fear of isolation - if they stop working, they'll have no one to talk to all day\n3. They've been invited to a reading group and volunteer work but avoid them because they feel they won't fit in\n4. Their son asked on a video call if they're seeing someone - this comment lingered with them\n5. They want to explore why saying \"I'm lonely\" feels so difficult, and why they keep pulling back when trying to meet people\n\nThis is a rich, layered disclosure. The client is showing:\n- Fear of loneliness/isolation\n- Avoidance patterns in social situations (anticipating rejection or not fitting in)\n- Vulnerability about aging and connection (the son's question about a romantic partner)\n- Self-awareness about their avoidance pattern\n- A desire to understand WHY they pull back\n\nWhat would be helpful here? The client has already done significant self-reflection. They're not just venting - they're asking to explore a pattern. I should:\n- Acknowledge the courage it took to articulate this\n- Reflect back what I'm hearing - the pattern of wanting connection but pulling back\n- Not rush to solutions (reading groups, volunteering, dating)\n- Not minimize the fear\n- Perhaps gently explore the \"pulling back\" moment - what happens right before they decide not to go?\n- One question at most\n\nI want to avoid:\n- \"That sounds really hard\" followed by a generic question\n- Jumping to \"have you tried...\" solutions\n- Making assumptions about why they pull back\n- Being overly analytical\n\nI think the most helpful response would be to reflect the tension I hear - between wanting connection and the pull to withdraw - and gently invite them to look at what happens in that moment of pulling back. The son's question seems significant too, but I shouldn't unpack everything at once. The core question they're asking is \"why do I pull back?\" - I can help them explore that.\n\nLet me write a natural, warm but not saccharine response in Korean.\n",
